# GoVecDB v1 — search path, end to end
#
# Every hop of the descent computes a distance against a RAM-resident vector,
# and the metadata attached at the end is RAM too: a search does no disk I/O
# at all. What it can wait on is a lock, so the two read locks it takes are
# drawn as the containers they are.

vars: {
  d2-config: {
    theme-id: 200
    layout-engine: elk
    pad: 40
  }
}


# Black canvas. Theme 200 makes default label/markdown text light;
# every explicit fill below is a dark surface chosen to keep its hue.
style.fill: "#000000"

direction: down

classes: {
  step: {
    style.fill: "#0D2137"
    style.stroke: "#4FC3F7"
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
  hot: {
    style.fill: "#332B0D"
    style.stroke: "#FFD54F"
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
  group: {
    style.fill: "#0C0C14"
    style.stroke: "#44445A"
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
  note: {
    shape: text
    style.font-size: 13
    style.italic: true
  }
}

start: "db.Search(SearchRequest)" {
  shape: circle
  style.fill: "#3A2B52"
  style.stroke: "#B39DDB"
}

s1: "1 · closed check, then validateSearch" {
  class: step
  body: |md
    `closed` atomic → `ErrClosed`
    query: right dimension, **every value finite**
    (one NaN poisons the ordering)
    `1 ≤ K ≤ maxK` · `0 ≤ Ef ≤ maxEf` · `0 ≤ TargetRecall < 1`
    `Filter.Validate()` **once**, here — never per node
    → `ErrInvalidFilter`
  |
}

s2: 2 · Choose ef {
  class: step
  body: |md
    `Ef == 0` means "choose it":
    `SuggestedEf(K, target)` — target defaults to **0.95**
    fitted `ef ∝ n^0.78 · k^0.2 · (16/M)^0.85`
    **× efScale** from the last `Calibrate` (1 until one runs)
    clamped to `maxEf`, then `max(ef, K)`
  |
}

s3: 3 · Build the predicate {
  class: step
  body: |md
    `allow(id) = store.Match(id, f.Match)`
    over ids, not metadata — the index never sees
    a second data model. `Match` does not copy:
    **0 allocs**, it runs once per candidate node
  |
}

reader: "db.applyMu.RLock — one hold spans traversal and metadata" {
  class: group

  s4: 4 · Borrow scratch, normalize {
    class: step
    body: |md
      `searchState` from a `sync.Pool`
      Cosine: copy into `st.queryBuf` and normalize —
      the caller's slice is never touched, and this
      runs **before** the graph lock: the buffer is ours
    |
  }

  hot_path: "g.mu.RLock — RAM only, parallel with other searches" {
    class: hot

    s5: 5 · Greedy descent, maxLevel → 1 {
      class: step
      body: |md
        `greedyClosest(st, cur, q, lc)`

        Step to the closest neighbor until none improves.
        **Unfiltered and tombstone-blind**: pure routing,
        the node it lands on is a seed, never an answer.
      |
    }

    s6: "6 · searchLayer(st, q, cur, ef, 0, allow)" {
      class: step
      body: |md
        Best-first on layer 0, the only layer holding every vector.

        **Gather · touch · score** per expansion: unvisited
        neighbors into `st.fresh`, one float per 128 B line
        each (`touchLines`) so all misses are in flight, then
        distances in the original order — 1.7–1.9×, bit-identical.

        **cands** (min-heap frontier) admits everything —
        a tombstone or a filtered-out node is still a bridge.
        **results** (max-heap, worst at top) admits only
        `admits()`: live **and** `allow(id)`.

        Stop when the nearest frontier node is farther
        than the worst of ef results.
      |
    }

    s5 -> s6: seed with best entry point
  }

  s7: 7 · Take top k {
    class: step
    body: |md
      `searchLayer` returns ascending, so `w[:k]`
      → `[]Result{ID, Distance}` — the graph's one
      allocation — then the adapter retypes to `[]Match`
    |
  }

  s8: 8 · Attach metadata {
    class: step
    body: |md
      `store.Get(id)` per match — an in-RAM `store.Map`,
      returned as a **copy** so a caller cannot mutate
      the store. Under the same `applyMu` hold as the
      traversal, so no result pairs `v2` with `md1`.
    |
  }

  s4 -> hot_path.s5
  hot_path.s6 -> s7 -> s8
}

lock_note: |md
  ### Readers never wait on a writer's I/O

  `applyMu` is held exclusively only for the apply itself —
  never across the WAL append or fsync, which sit under
  `writeMu`, a lock no reader takes. So the most a search
  waits for is **one apply**; large batches apply in groups
  of `applyGroup` and release between them.

  Snapshots hold `writeMu`, not these locks — searches keep
  running through one.
| {
  class: note
}

alloc_note: |md
  ### Why the graph search is 1 alloc/op

  - **visited set** — generation-stamped `[]uint32`, not a
    `map[int]struct{}`. Clearing is `gen++`, O(1), no alloc.
  - **heaps** — plain `[]candidate` with hand-written
    sift up/down. `container/heap` passes values as `any`,
    which boxes every candidate.
  - **searchLayer's output** lives in the state too (`st.found`).
  - The one allocation is the `[]Result` handed back
    (`BenchmarkSearch`, and `BenchmarkSearchFilter` filtered).
    The DB adds the `[]Match` and the metadata copies.

  **This scratch is per-traversal, not graph-owned.** It comes
  from a pooled `searchState`, which is what lets `Search` hold
  only `RLock` and run in parallel — graph-owned buffers would
  make concurrent searches corrupt each other.
| {
  class: note
}

done: "[]Match — ascending, smaller = closer" {
  shape: circle
  style.fill: "#1B4D2E"
  style.stroke: "#66BB6A"
}

recall_note: |md
  ### ef is the accuracy dial

  Bigger = wider layer-0 search = higher recall, slower.
  **Recall at a fixed ef falls as N or dimension grows**,
  so leave `Ef` at 0 and set `TargetRecall` instead.
  `Calibrate` (leave-one-out, background, re-run when the
  live count doubles or halves) corrects the formula for
  how hard *this* data is.

  Locked baselines: **recall@10 = 0.999** at dim 32,
  **0.988** at dim 768, against brute force in `graph_test.go`.

  `M` is structural — changing it requires a rebuild.
| {
  class: note
}

antipattern: REJECTED DESIGNS {
  style.fill: "#3B1215"
  style.stroke: "#EF5350"
  style.stroke-width: 3

  body: |md
    ### "Filter the results afterwards"

    Returns fewer than k. Measured at one-in-fifty
    selectivity: **10 results in-traversal, 2 post-filtered**.
    Same mistake as filtering tombstones at the end —
    which is why `admits()` holds both checks.

    ### "Get indices from HNSW, then fetch vectors from disk"

    The graph needs a vector at **every hop** to compute the
    next distance — hundreds of lookups per query. Backing
    vectors with disk is not a slower HNSW, it is a different
    and much worse algorithm. Disk-friendly traversal is
    **DiskANN** — a different index, out of scope.
  |
}

start -> s1 -> s2 -> s3 -> reader.s4
reader.s8 -> done

# --- annotations, drawn faint so they never compete with the flow ---
reader -> lock_note: {
  style.stroke-dash: 4
  style.opacity: 0.7
  style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
}
reader.hot_path -> alloc_note: {
  style.stroke-dash: 4
  style.opacity: 0.7
  style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
}
s2 -> recall_note: {
  style.stroke-dash: 4
  style.opacity: 0.7
  style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
}
reader.hot_path.s6 -> antipattern: {
  style.stroke-dash: 4
  style.opacity: 0.7
  style.stroke: "#6E6E6E"
}
